6 & 7. Types of DI and Their Pros/Cons
Spring mainly supports three types of Dependency Injection: Constructor Injection, Setter Injection, and Field Injection. Here is how we choose between them in production:
1. Constructor Injection (Strongly Recommended)
How it looks: Dependencies are passed directly through the class constructor.
Pros: It allows us to mark our fields as final, ensuring immutability so dependencies can't be modified after startup. It also guarantees compile-time safety; you can't create an instance of the 
class in a 'half-baked' or uninitialized state without passing its required dependencies.
Cons: If a class has too many dependencies, the constructor parameter list becomes bloated and messy (though this is actually a good warning sign that your class is doing too much and needs to be refactored).
2. Setter Injection
How it looks: Dependencies are passed via public setXXX() methods marked with @Autowired.
Pros: It's highly flexible. It allows you to inject or swap out optional or fluid dependencies that might change at runtime. It's also great for resolving circular dependencies.
Cons: You lose immutability because fields cannot be final. Additionally, an object might be exposed in an unstable, incomplete state before the setter method is fully executed.
3. Field Injection (Highly Discouraged)
How it looks: You just slap @Autowired directly on a private variable.
Pros: It’s incredibly short, clean, and fast to write.
Cons (Make sure to point this out to impress the interviewer!): It is a major anti-pattern. First, it tightly couples your class to the Spring container. If you want to write a simple, vanilla Java 
unit test using Mockito, you can't easily inject your mocks because the field is private and lacks a constructor or setter. Second, it hides the class’s dependency footprint, making it easy to 
accidentally build a massive, unmaintainable class.


8. @Component vs @Bean
Both annotations are used to register a Java object as a Spring Bean in the IoC container, but they target completely different locations and use cases:
@Component is a CLASS-level annotation: You use this on classes that you own and write from scratch. You put @Component at the very top of your custom class, and Spring's component scanner automatically 
finds it, instantiates it, and manages it.
@Bean is a METHOD-level annotation: You use this inside a configuration class (@Configuration). It is primarily used when you want to register an instance of a third-party library (where you don't own 
the source code and cannot modify the class to add @Component). For example, if you want to use Google's Gson or a RedisTemplate, you write a method that manually executes return new Gson();, and you 
mark that method with @Bean. Spring then captures whatever that method returns and places it into the container.


9. What are @Configuration and @ComponentScan?
These two annotations work together to build the baseline architecture of a Spring application:
@Configuration: This tells Spring that this class isn't a standard business service, but a 'blueprint configuration sheet.' It indicates that the class contains methods annotated with @Bean that need to 
be processed to initialize global settings or third-party integrations.
@ComponentScan: This acts as Spring’s radar system. Since we have annotations like @Component and @Service scattered across dozens of packages, Spring needs to know where to look. @ComponentScan tells the 
framework exactly which base packages to search through so it can discover and register those classes.
Note: In modern Spring Boot applications, we rarely have to type these explicitly because @SpringBootApplication automatically includes both under the hood, defaulting to scanning the current package and 
all of its sub-packages.


10. @Controller vs @RestController
The difference between these two boils down to traditional server-side rendering versus modern decoupled API architectures:
@Controller: This is the traditional MVC controller. When a method in a @Controller returns a String, Spring treats that String as a View Name. It tells the View Resolver to look for a matching template 
file, like index.html or a JSP page, to render a UI.
@RestController: This is purpose-built for RESTful Web Services and decoupled Front-End/Back-End projects. It is actually a combination of @Controller and @ResponseBody. When a method here returns an 
object or a String, it doesn't look for an HTML page; instead, it automatically serializes that data directly into JSON format and dumps it straight into the HTTP response body.


11. @Controller vs @Service vs @Repository
Under the hood, these three annotations are technically identical. If you look at their source code, they are all meta-annotated with @Component. This means that from Spring's perspective, they all do the same 
basic job: registering a class as a bean.
However, we separate them into three distinct types to enforce a clean three-tier architecture and provide meaningful semantics:
@Controller (Presentation Layer): This handles incoming HTTP requests from the client, validates basic inputs, and routes traffic.
@Service (Business Logic Layer): This is the 'brain' of the application. It handles heavy calculations, orchestrates workflows, evaluates business rules, and manages transactional boundaries.
@Repository (Data Access Layer): This directly interfaces with the database (DAO). Plus, it has a cool hidden feature: it automatically catches low-level database-specific exceptions and translates them into 
Spring’s uniform, readable DataAccessException hierarchy.


12 & 13. Spring Bean Scopes: Singleton vs. Prototype
Bean scope dictates how long a bean lives, when a new instance is created, and who gets to share it. The two foundational scopes are Singleton and Prototype:
Singleton (Default): The IoC container creates exactly one single instance of this bean globally. Every time that bean is injected or requested anywhere in the application, Spring hands out a reference to that 
exact same object in memory.
Prototype: This operates on a 'create-on-demand' basis. Every single time a class requests a prototype bean, Spring spins up a brand-new, independent instance, and states are completely isolated from one another.
If the interviewer asks about Thread Safety: Singletons are NOT inherently thread-safe. Because every user thread shares the same instance, if your singleton contains mutable member variables (state), you will 
run into massive race conditions. Therefore, we always design our standard Spring singletons to be stateless—meaning they only contain methods and read-only dependencies.


14. Real-World Use Cases for the 4 Main Scopes
1. Singleton Scope
Stateless Service/Repository Classes: Your standard UserServiceImpl or OrderRepository. They hold no client-specific data; they just process logic, making them perfect for global sharing to save memory.
Database Connection Pools (DataSource): You only want one central pool managing connections to your database instance across the entire app.
Application Configuration Beans: A bean that reads global system environment variables from application.properties.
2. Prototype Scope
Multi-threaded Worker Tasks: Background thread workers that need to maintain their own local step counters or processing states without bleeding into other threads.
Stateful Input Form Validators: A temporary object that captures a user's multi-step wizard inputs to perform calculations and is then instantly discarded.
Dynamic Document Exporters: A PDF or Excel file generator bean that needs to hold temporary custom styling, file streams, and datasets unique to a single download request.
3. Request Scope (Web Only)
HTTP Request Logging/Tracing: Storing a unique Trace ID generated when a web request hits the server. Every service handling that request can log using that specific ID, and the bean dies the millisecond the request ends.
User-Specific Language/Locale Storage: Storing the client's preferred language parsed from the Accept-Language header for a single page render.
API Payload Buffering: Storing transient request metadata to prevent duplicate API submissions within a tight time window.
4. Session Scope (Web Only)
E-Commerce Shopping Cart: Keeping track of the items a user adds to their cart as they browse through different pages, tabs, and products before checking out.
User Authentication Profiles: Storing a logged-in user's profile info, permissions, and roles so you don't have to constantly query the database on every single page click.
Multi-Page Registration Wizards: Caching a user's progress across a 4-step registration form before hitting the final submit button.


15. How to explain "Session vs. Cookie"
Because HTTP is fundamentally a stateless protocol, the server forgets who the client is the split-second a response is sent. To fix this and maintain a user's logged-in state, we use Cookies and Sessions as a 
tag-team. However, they store data in completely opposite locations:
Cookies are stored on the CLIENT side (the browser): Think of a cookie as a physical membership card the server hands to you. Every time your browser makes a new request to that website, it automatically attaches 
that card to the HTTP request header. Because it lives on the user's machine, it has a size limit (usually 4KB) and is highly insecure—anyone can open F12 Developer Tools and alter or forge a cookie value.
Sessions are stored on the SERVER side (in memory or a Redis cache): Think of a session as the internal secure database file kept behind the counter at the business. It can hold a massive amount of highly sensitive 
data (like banking info, user IDs, or access tokens). Because it never leaves the server, it is extremely secure.
How they execute a handshake together:
When a user logs in successfully, the server creates a Session in its memory to hold their account details.
The server generates a unique, unguessable string tied to that session called a Session ID.
The server sends this Session ID back to the browser inside an HTTP header, telling the browser to save it as a Cookie.
On every subsequent click, the browser automatically attaches that Session ID cookie to the request.
The server reads the ID, matches it with the secure Session file in its database, and instantly knows exactly who the user is.
To summarize: The Cookie is the key, and the Session is the locked treasure chest on the server.
